---
title: WebSockets
id: websockets
description: "Run resumable streams over one full-duplex WebSocket instead of a request per turn: toWebSocketStream/toWebSocketResponse on the server, webSocket() on the client, resume via a URL query, and hosting on Node or Cloudflare."
keywords:
  - websocket transport
  - toWebSocketStream
  - toWebSocketResponse
  - resumeWebSocketStream
  - resumeWebSocketResponse
  - full-duplex chat
  - conversation-scoped socket
  - websocket reconnect
  - websocket heartbeat
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSE and NDJSON open one connection per turn. A WebSocket is different: one\nsocket stays open for the whole conversation, carries every turn, and lets the\nserver push chunks without waiting on a request. Reach for it when you want a\npersistent channel instead of a request-per-message model: a WebSocket\ngateway you already run, a mobile client that wants to avoid repeated\nhandshakes, or a UI that needs the server to push outside of a reply (this page\nonly covers the request/reply case; server-initiated pushes need your own\nframing on top)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By the end of this page you can run a chat turn over a socket that resumes\nafter a drop, without re-running the model."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-shape-of-it","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shape of it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One socket, many turns:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client opens the socket once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each user message becomes one JSON frame sent over the socket (the same\nshape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server runs one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" turn per frame and streams the chunks back\nover the same socket."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The socket stays open after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" (waiting for the next frame)\nuntil the client closes it, an idle timeout fires, or the process aborts."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-onrun","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRun"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pair the socket you already accepted with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":". It decodes\ninbound frames, starts one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" turn per frame through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRun"},{"type":"text","value":", and pumps\nthe resulting chunks back out:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, memoryStream, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Bridge the per-turn AbortSignal WsRunContext hands you into the\n// AbortController chat() expects.\nfunction abortControllerFromSignal(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  if (signal.aborted) controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  else\n    signal.addEventListener('abort', () => controller.abort(signal.reason), {\n      once: true,\n    })\n  return controller\n}\n\n// `socket` is a WHATWG-shaped server socket you already accepted (see\n// \"Hosting\" below for where it comes from on Node vs Cloudflare) and\n// `request` is the original handshake request.\nexport function handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    // Per-turn durability, keyed by the frame's runId (see \"Durability is\n    // per turn\" below).\n    durability: (ctx) => memoryStream(ctx.request),\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId, signal }) =>\n      chat({\n        adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n        messages,\n        threadId,\n        runId,\n        abortController: abortControllerFromSignal(signal),\n      }),\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRun"},{"type":"text","value":" receives one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WsRunContext"},{"type":"text","value":" per inbound frame:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it is"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIMessage[]"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ModelMessage[]"},{"type":"text","value":", decoded from the frame."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The AG-UI identifiers for this turn."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forwardedProps"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any extra data the client sent with the frame."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A synthetic per-turn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Request"},{"type":"text","value":" carrying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId="},{"type":"text","value":" in its URL, for keying a durability adapter."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aborts when the socket closes, or when this turn receives an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort"},{"type":"text","value":" frame (see below). It does not abort other turns on the same socket."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" entirely for a socket that doesn't need to survive a drop.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" still pumps chunks, it just can't replay them on\nreconnect."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-websocket","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SubscribeConnectionAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" (also exported\nfrom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-solid"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-vue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-svelte"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-angular"},{"type":"text","value":"). It opens the socket lazily on the first\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage"},{"type":"text","value":", and reuses it for every later turn in the conversation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, webSocket } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst connection = webSocket('/api/chat-ws')\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({ connection })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Hello')}>Send</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing else changes: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":", tool calls, and\npersistence all work the same as with any other connection adapter. Options:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it does"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"protocols"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebSocket subprotocol(s), passed straight to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":" constructor."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"body"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static extra fields merged into every outgoing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunAgentInput"},{"type":"text","value":" frame (same as the HTTP adapters' "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"body"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconnect"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnect bounds ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAttempts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delayMs"},{"type":"text","value":"), shared semantics with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" — see "},{"type":"link","href":"./advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketImpl"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Override the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation (tests, non-browser runtimes)."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../transports/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transports"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfits among the other adapters."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wire-protocol","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire protocol"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direction"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Frame"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client → server"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunAgentInput"},{"type":"text","value":"-shaped JSON object (same shape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" turn."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client → server"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ \"type\": \"abort\", \"runId\": \"…\" }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Abort one in-flight turn (see below)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server → client"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ \"id\": \"…\", \"chunk\": <StreamChunk> }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One chunk, tagged with a durability offset (only when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" is configured)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server → client"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A bare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamChunk"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One chunk, untagged (no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" configured)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server → client"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ \"type\": \"ping\" }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heartbeat. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" drops these automatically; a hand-rolled client should ignore anything with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type: \"ping\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two server→client shapes are unambiguous: the envelope never has a\ntop-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type"},{"type":"text","value":", and every bare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" does."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resume-rides-in-the-url-not-a-header","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume rides in the URL, not a header"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSE and NDJSON resume with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":" header, because a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":"/XHR\nrequest can set arbitrary headers before it opens. A browser's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":"\nconstructor cannot set custom headers on the handshake, so the offset instead\nrides in the URL: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" handles this for you. If the socket drops before a run's\nterminal chunk ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":") and that run was durable\n(offset-tagged envelopes), it reopens at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId=&offset="},{"type":"text","value":" and de-dupes the\nreplayed boundary, the same reconnect guarantee "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" gives\nyou, just carried differently on the wire. A run that never emitted an offset\n(no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" configured) has nothing to resume from: the drop surfaces as\na connection error instead of retrying forever."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, a URL carrying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?offset="},{"type":"text","value":" is a resume, not a fresh turn. Route\nit to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":", a read-only replay of the durability log with\nno model call:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function handleResumeSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  resumeWebSocketStream(socket, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" closes the socket with code "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1008"},{"type":"text","value":" if the request\ncarries no resume offset: there's nothing to replay."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"durability-is-per-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durability is per turn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A conversation-scoped socket carries many turns, so its durability adapter\ncan't be built once for the whole connection. Each turn needs its own log,\nkeyed by that turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". That's why "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis a factory, not a value: it receives the per-turn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx"},{"type":"text","value":" and reads\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.request"},{"type":"text","value":", whose URL already carries that turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId="},{"type":"text","value":"\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" key off it automatically)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"abort-a-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Abort a turn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ type: 'abort', runId }"},{"type":"text","value":" frame aborts only that turn's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRun"},{"type":"text","value":" iteration\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.signal"},{"type":"text","value":" fires); the socket itself stays open for the next turn. The\nbuilt-in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" client adapter sends this frame when the run's abort\nsignal fires ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":"), so the server actually stops generating\ninstead of running the model call to completion against a client that stopped\nlistening. A hand-rolled client should send the same frame to cancel a turn."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Closing the whole socket aborts every turn still in flight on it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"heartbeat-and-idle-timeout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heartbeat and idle timeout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" sends a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ type: 'ping' }"},{"type":"text","value":" frame every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"heartbeatMs"},{"type":"text","value":"\n(default 30 seconds) to keep the connection alive through proxies that drop\nidle sockets. It closes the socket if no inbound frame arrives for\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idleTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 5 minutes); a heartbeat itself doesn't count as\nactivity, only a client-sent frame does. The idle timeout never fires while a\nturn is still streaming, so a long single generation (an agentic loop, a\nturn longer than the timeout) is never cut off:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    heartbeatMs: 15_000,\n    idleTimeoutMs: 60_000,\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hosting-on-node","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hosting on Node"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Plain Node (and anywhere else without a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketPair"},{"type":"text","value":") has no\nbuilt-in way to accept a WebSocket upgrade, so you do it yourself and hand the\nresult to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":". The pattern: hook the HTTP server's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upgrade"},{"type":"text","value":"\nevent, accept the socket with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/websockets/ws","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ws"}]},{"type":"text","value":"'s\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })"},{"type":"text","value":", and pass the resulting socket straight\nthrough. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ws"},{"type":"text","value":"'s socket already implements the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close"},{"type":"text","value":"/\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addEventListener"},{"type":"text","value":" surface "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketLike"},{"type":"text","value":" needs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { WebSocketServer } from 'ws'\nimport { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { Plugin } from 'vite'\nimport { handleChatSocket } from './handle-chat-socket'\n\nconst WS_PATH = '/api/chat-ws'\n\nexport function webSocketChatPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'websocket-chat-plugin',\n    configureServer(server) {\n      if (!server.httpServer) return\n      const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })\n\n      server.httpServer.on('upgrade', (req, socket, head) => {\n        const url = new URL(req.url ?? '/', `http://${req.headers.host}`)\n        if (url.pathname !== WS_PATH) return\n\n        wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {\n          // `ws`'s socket satisfies WebSocketLike structurally — pass it\n          // straight through, no adapter needed.\n          const request = new Request(url)\n          if (url.searchParams.get('offset') !== null) {\n            resumeWebSocketStream(ws, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n          } else {\n            handleChatSocket(ws, request)\n          }\n        })\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the same pattern used by the working\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/examples/ts-react-chat/src/lib/websocket-chat-plugin.ts","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/ts-react-chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nWebSocket example and by the e2e suite's\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/testing/e2e/src/lib/durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe same wiring pattern applies elsewhere: accept the platform's socket, then\ncall "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":" with it. A socket from\nDeno's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Deno.upgradeWebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":" is WHATWG-shaped and satisfies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketLike"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndirectly; Bun's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerWebSocket"},{"type":"text","value":" (handler-object API) needs a small adapter\nfirst."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hosting-on-cloudflare","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hosting on Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workers (and Durable Objects) expose a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketPair"},{"type":"text","value":", so\nyou don't upgrade anything by hand. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" creates the pair,\naccepts the server half, wires it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toWebSocketStream"},{"type":"text","value":", and returns the 101\nupgrade "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" for you:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toWebSocketResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport default {\n  fetch(request: Request): Response {\n    return toWebSocketResponse(request, {\n      onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n        chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    })\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeWebSocketResponse({ adapter })"},{"type":"text","value":" is the matching read-only wrapper for a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?offset="},{"type":"text","value":" resume. Both throw with a message pointing at their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*Stream"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncounterpart if called somewhere without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WebSocketPair"},{"type":"text","value":" (Node, for instance),\nso there's no way to accidentally ship the Cloudflare wrapper to a runtime\nthat can't upgrade a socket itself."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"producer-vs-socket-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Producer vs. socket lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same caveat that applies to SSE and NDJSON applies here: with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":", the run's producer and the delivery socket live in the same\nprocess. If that socket drops, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call backing it aborts too, so\nreconnecting only replays what was already logged rather than resuming a run\nstill in progress. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" decouples the two (the producer runs\nagainst a backend, not the client's socket), so a drop there can reconnect to\na run that's still actively producing. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./advanced#memorystream-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"memoryStream in production"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the\nfull explanation. It applies to WebSockets exactly as written there."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the durability adapter contract this transport\nreuses."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced"}]},{"type":"text","value":": reconnection bounding, offset ownership, and\nCloudflare Durable Streams deployment, shared across every transport."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../transports/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transports"}]},{"type":"text","value":": where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfits among the other client adapters."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-shape-of-it","text":"The shape of it","level":2},{"id":"server-onrun","text":"Server: onRun","level":2},{"id":"client-websocket","text":"Client: webSocket()","level":2},{"id":"wire-protocol","text":"Wire protocol","level":2},{"id":"resume-rides-in-the-url-not-a-header","text":"Resume rides in the URL, not a header","level":2},{"id":"durability-is-per-turn","text":"Durability is per turn","level":2},{"id":"abort-a-turn","text":"Abort a turn","level":2},{"id":"heartbeat-and-idle-timeout","text":"Heartbeat and idle timeout","level":2},{"id":"hosting-on-node","text":"Hosting on Node","level":2},{"id":"hosting-on-cloudflare","text":"Hosting on Cloudflare","level":2},{"id":"producer-vs-socket-lifecycle","text":"Producer vs. socket lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nSSE and NDJSON open one connection per turn. A WebSocket is different: one\nsocket stays open for the whole conversation, carries every turn, and lets the\nserver push chunks without waiting on a request. Reach for it when you want a\npersistent channel instead of a request-per-message model: a WebSocket\ngateway you already run, a mobile client that wants to avoid repeated\nhandshakes, or a UI that needs the server to push outside of a reply (this page\nonly covers the request/reply case; server-initiated pushes need your own\nframing on top).\n\nBy the end of this page you can run a chat turn over a socket that resumes\nafter a drop, without re-running the model.\n\n## The shape of it\n\nOne socket, many turns:\n\n1. The client opens the socket once.\n2. Each user message becomes one JSON frame sent over the socket (the same\n   shape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body).\n3. The server runs one `chat()` turn per frame and streams the chunks back\n   over the same socket.\n4. The socket stays open after `RUN_FINISHED` (waiting for the next frame)\n   until the client closes it, an idle timeout fires, or the process aborts.\n\n## Server: `onRun`\n\nPair the socket you already accepted with `toWebSocketStream`. It decodes\ninbound frames, starts one `chat()` turn per frame through `onRun`, and pumps\nthe resulting chunks back out:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, memoryStream, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Bridge the per-turn AbortSignal WsRunContext hands you into the\n// AbortController chat() expects.\nfunction abortControllerFromSignal(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  if (signal.aborted) controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  else\n    signal.addEventListener('abort', () => controller.abort(signal.reason), {\n      once: true,\n    })\n  return controller\n}\n\n// `socket` is a WHATWG-shaped server socket you already accepted (see\n// \"Hosting\" below for where it comes from on Node vs Cloudflare) and\n// `request` is the original handshake request.\nexport function handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    // Per-turn durability, keyed by the frame's runId (see \"Durability is\n    // per turn\" below).\n    durability: (ctx) => memoryStream(ctx.request),\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId, signal }) =>\n      chat({\n        adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n        messages,\n        threadId,\n        runId,\n        abortController: abortControllerFromSignal(signal),\n      }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`onRun` receives one `WsRunContext` per inbound frame:\n\n| Field | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `messages` | The turn's `UIMessage[]` / `ModelMessage[]`, decoded from the frame. |\n| `threadId` / `runId` | The AG-UI identifiers for this turn. |\n| `forwardedProps` | Any extra data the client sent with the frame. |\n| `request` | A synthetic per-turn `Request` carrying `?runId=` in its URL, for keying a durability adapter. |\n| `signal` | Aborts when the socket closes, or when this turn receives an `abort` frame (see below). It does not abort other turns on the same socket. |\n\nSkip `durability` entirely for a socket that doesn't need to survive a drop.\n`toWebSocketStream` still pumps chunks, it just can't replay them on\nreconnect.\n\n## Client: `webSocket()`\n\n`webSocket()` is a `SubscribeConnectionAdapter` for `useChat` (also exported\nfrom `@tanstack/ai-solid`, `@tanstack/ai-vue`, `@tanstack/ai-svelte`, and\n`@tanstack/ai-angular`). It opens the socket lazily on the first\n`sendMessage`, and reuses it for every later turn in the conversation:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, webSocket } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst connection = webSocket('/api/chat-ws')\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({ connection })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Hello')}>Send</button>\n}\n```\n\nNothing else changes: `messages`, `sendMessage`, `stop()`, tool calls, and\npersistence all work the same as with any other connection adapter. Options:\n\n| Option | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `protocols` | WebSocket subprotocol(s), passed straight to the `WebSocket` constructor. |\n| `body` | Static extra fields merged into every outgoing `RunAgentInput` frame (same as the HTTP adapters' `body`). |\n| `reconnect` | Reconnect bounds (`maxAttempts`, `delayMs`), shared semantics with `fetchServerSentEvents` — see [Advanced](./advanced). |\n| `WebSocketImpl` | Override the `WebSocket` implementation (tests, non-browser runtimes). |\n\nSee [Transports](../transports/overview) for where `webSocket()`\nfits among the other adapters.\n\n## Wire protocol\n\n| Direction | Frame | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Client → server | A `RunAgentInput`-shaped JSON object (same shape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body) | Start one `chat()` turn. |\n| Client → server | `{ \"type\": \"abort\", \"runId\": \"…\" }` | Abort one in-flight turn (see below). |\n| Server → client | `{ \"id\": \"…\", \"chunk\": <StreamChunk> }` | One chunk, tagged with a durability offset (only when `durability` is configured). |\n| Server → client | A bare `StreamChunk` | One chunk, untagged (no `durability` configured). |\n| Server → client | `{ \"type\": \"ping\" }` | Heartbeat. `webSocket()` drops these automatically; a hand-rolled client should ignore anything with `type: \"ping\"`. |\n\nThe two server→client shapes are unambiguous: the envelope never has a\ntop-level `type`, and every bare `StreamChunk` does.\n\n## Resume rides in the URL, not a header\n\nSSE and NDJSON resume with the `Last-Event-ID` header, because a `fetch`/XHR\nrequest can set arbitrary headers before it opens. A browser's `WebSocket`\nconstructor cannot set custom headers on the handshake, so the offset instead\nrides in the URL: `?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>`.\n\n`webSocket()` handles this for you. If the socket drops before a run's\nterminal chunk (`RUN_FINISHED` / `RUN_ERROR`) and that run was durable\n(offset-tagged envelopes), it reopens at `?runId=&offset=` and de-dupes the\nreplayed boundary, the same reconnect guarantee `fetchServerSentEvents` gives\nyou, just carried differently on the wire. A run that never emitted an offset\n(no `durability` configured) has nothing to resume from: the drop surfaces as\na connection error instead of retrying forever.\n\nOn the server, a URL carrying `?offset=` is a resume, not a fresh turn. Route\nit to `resumeWebSocketStream`, a read-only replay of the durability log with\nno model call:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function handleResumeSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  resumeWebSocketStream(socket, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}\n```\n\n`resumeWebSocketStream` closes the socket with code `1008` if the request\ncarries no resume offset: there's nothing to replay.\n\n## Durability is per turn\n\nA conversation-scoped socket carries many turns, so its durability adapter\ncan't be built once for the whole connection. Each turn needs its own log,\nkeyed by that turn's `runId`. That's why `durability` in `toWebSocketStream`\nis a factory, not a value: it receives the per-turn `ctx` and reads\n`ctx.request`, whose URL already carries that turn's `?runId=`\n(`memoryStream`/`durableStream` key off it automatically).\n\n## Abort a turn\n\nAn `{ type: 'abort', runId }` frame aborts only that turn's `onRun` iteration\n(`ctx.signal` fires); the socket itself stays open for the next turn. The\nbuilt-in `webSocket()` client adapter sends this frame when the run's abort\nsignal fires (`stop()` in `useChat`), so the server actually stops generating\ninstead of running the model call to completion against a client that stopped\nlistening. A hand-rolled client should send the same frame to cancel a turn.\n\nClosing the whole socket aborts every turn still in flight on it.\n\n## Heartbeat and idle timeout\n\n`toWebSocketStream` sends a `{ type: 'ping' }` frame every `heartbeatMs`\n(default 30 seconds) to keep the connection alive through proxies that drop\nidle sockets. It closes the socket if no inbound frame arrives for\n`idleTimeoutMs` (default 5 minutes); a heartbeat itself doesn't count as\nactivity, only a client-sent frame does. The idle timeout never fires while a\nturn is still streaming, so a long single generation (an agentic loop, a\nturn longer than the timeout) is never cut off:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    heartbeatMs: 15_000,\n    idleTimeoutMs: 60_000,\n  })\n}\n```\n\n## Hosting on Node\n\nPlain Node (and anywhere else without a global `WebSocketPair`) has no\nbuilt-in way to accept a WebSocket upgrade, so you do it yourself and hand the\nresult to `toWebSocketStream`. The pattern: hook the HTTP server's `upgrade`\nevent, accept the socket with [`ws`](https://github.com/websockets/ws)'s\n`WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })`, and pass the resulting socket straight\nthrough. `ws`'s socket already implements the `send`/`close`/\n`addEventListener` surface `WebSocketLike` needs:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { WebSocketServer } from 'ws'\nimport { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { Plugin } from 'vite'\nimport { handleChatSocket } from './handle-chat-socket'\n\nconst WS_PATH = '/api/chat-ws'\n\nexport function webSocketChatPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'websocket-chat-plugin',\n    configureServer(server) {\n      if (!server.httpServer) return\n      const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })\n\n      server.httpServer.on('upgrade', (req, socket, head) => {\n        const url = new URL(req.url ?? '/', `http://${req.headers.host}`)\n        if (url.pathname !== WS_PATH) return\n\n        wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {\n          // `ws`'s socket satisfies WebSocketLike structurally — pass it\n          // straight through, no adapter needed.\n          const request = new Request(url)\n          if (url.searchParams.get('offset') !== null) {\n            resumeWebSocketStream(ws, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n          } else {\n            handleChatSocket(ws, request)\n          }\n        })\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is the same pattern used by the working\n[`examples/ts-react-chat`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/examples/ts-react-chat/src/lib/websocket-chat-plugin.ts)\nWebSocket example and by the e2e suite's\n[`durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/testing/e2e/src/lib/durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts).\nThe same wiring pattern applies elsewhere: accept the platform's socket, then\ncall `toWebSocketStream` / `resumeWebSocketStream` with it. A socket from\nDeno's `Deno.upgradeWebSocket` is WHATWG-shaped and satisfies `WebSocketLike`\ndirectly; Bun's `ServerWebSocket` (handler-object API) needs a small adapter\nfirst.\n\n## Hosting on Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare Workers (and Durable Objects) expose a global `WebSocketPair`, so\nyou don't upgrade anything by hand. `toWebSocketResponse` creates the pair,\naccepts the server half, wires it to `toWebSocketStream`, and returns the 101\nupgrade `Response` for you:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toWebSocketResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport default {\n  fetch(request: Request): Response {\n    return toWebSocketResponse(request, {\n      onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n        chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    })\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`resumeWebSocketResponse({ adapter })` is the matching read-only wrapper for a\n`?offset=` resume. Both throw with a message pointing at their `*Stream`\ncounterpart if called somewhere without `WebSocketPair` (Node, for instance),\nso there's no way to accidentally ship the Cloudflare wrapper to a runtime\nthat can't upgrade a socket itself.\n\n## Producer vs. socket lifecycle\n\nThe same caveat that applies to SSE and NDJSON applies here: with\n`memoryStream`, the run's producer and the delivery socket live in the same\nprocess. If that socket drops, the `chat()` call backing it aborts too, so\nreconnecting only replays what was already logged rather than resuming a run\nstill in progress. `durableStream` decouples the two (the producer runs\nagainst a backend, not the client's socket), so a drop there can reconnect to\na run that's still actively producing. See\n[memoryStream in production](./advanced#memorystream-in-production) for the\nfull explanation. It applies to WebSockets exactly as written there.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [Overview](./overview): the durability adapter contract this transport\n  reuses.\n- [Advanced](./advanced): reconnection bounding, offset ownership, and\n  Cloudflare Durable Streams deployment, shared across every transport.\n- [Transports](../transports/overview): where `webSocket()`\n  fits among the other client adapters.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: WebSockets\nid: websockets\ndescription: \"Run resumable streams over one full-duplex WebSocket instead of a request per turn: toWebSocketStream/toWebSocketResponse on the server, webSocket() on the client, resume via a URL query, and hosting on Node or Cloudflare.\"\nkeywords:\n  - websocket transport\n  - toWebSocketStream\n  - toWebSocketResponse\n  - resumeWebSocketStream\n  - resumeWebSocketResponse\n  - full-duplex chat\n  - conversation-scoped socket\n  - websocket reconnect\n  - websocket heartbeat\n---\n\nSSE and NDJSON open one connection per turn. A WebSocket is different: one\nsocket stays open for the whole conversation, carries every turn, and lets the\nserver push chunks without waiting on a request. Reach for it when you want a\npersistent channel instead of a request-per-message model: a WebSocket\ngateway you already run, a mobile client that wants to avoid repeated\nhandshakes, or a UI that needs the server to push outside of a reply (this page\nonly covers the request/reply case; server-initiated pushes need your own\nframing on top).\n\nBy the end of this page you can run a chat turn over a socket that resumes\nafter a drop, without re-running the model.\n\n## The shape of it\n\nOne socket, many turns:\n\n1. The client opens the socket once.\n2. Each user message becomes one JSON frame sent over the socket (the same\n   shape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body).\n3. The server runs one `chat()` turn per frame and streams the chunks back\n   over the same socket.\n4. The socket stays open after `RUN_FINISHED` (waiting for the next frame)\n   until the client closes it, an idle timeout fires, or the process aborts.\n\n## Server: `onRun`\n\nPair the socket you already accepted with `toWebSocketStream`. It decodes\ninbound frames, starts one `chat()` turn per frame through `onRun`, and pumps\nthe resulting chunks back out:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, memoryStream, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Bridge the per-turn AbortSignal WsRunContext hands you into the\n// AbortController chat() expects.\nfunction abortControllerFromSignal(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  if (signal.aborted) controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  else\n    signal.addEventListener('abort', () => controller.abort(signal.reason), {\n      once: true,\n    })\n  return controller\n}\n\n// `socket` is a WHATWG-shaped server socket you already accepted (see\n// \"Hosting\" below for where it comes from on Node vs Cloudflare) and\n// `request` is the original handshake request.\nexport function handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    // Per-turn durability, keyed by the frame's runId (see \"Durability is\n    // per turn\" below).\n    durability: (ctx) => memoryStream(ctx.request),\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId, signal }) =>\n      chat({\n        adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n        messages,\n        threadId,\n        runId,\n        abortController: abortControllerFromSignal(signal),\n      }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`onRun` receives one `WsRunContext` per inbound frame:\n\n| Field | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `messages` | The turn's `UIMessage[]` / `ModelMessage[]`, decoded from the frame. |\n| `threadId` / `runId` | The AG-UI identifiers for this turn. |\n| `forwardedProps` | Any extra data the client sent with the frame. |\n| `request` | A synthetic per-turn `Request` carrying `?runId=` in its URL, for keying a durability adapter. |\n| `signal` | Aborts when the socket closes, or when this turn receives an `abort` frame (see below). It does not abort other turns on the same socket. |\n\nSkip `durability` entirely for a socket that doesn't need to survive a drop.\n`toWebSocketStream` still pumps chunks, it just can't replay them on\nreconnect.\n\n## Client: `webSocket()`\n\n`webSocket()` is a `SubscribeConnectionAdapter` for `useChat` (also exported\nfrom `@tanstack/ai-solid`, `@tanstack/ai-vue`, `@tanstack/ai-svelte`, and\n`@tanstack/ai-angular`). It opens the socket lazily on the first\n`sendMessage`, and reuses it for every later turn in the conversation:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, webSocket } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst connection = webSocket('/api/chat-ws')\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({ connection })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Hello')}>Send</button>\n}\n```\n\nNothing else changes: `messages`, `sendMessage`, `stop()`, tool calls, and\npersistence all work the same as with any other connection adapter. Options:\n\n| Option | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `protocols` | WebSocket subprotocol(s), passed straight to the `WebSocket` constructor. |\n| `body` | Static extra fields merged into every outgoing `RunAgentInput` frame (same as the HTTP adapters' `body`). |\n| `reconnect` | Reconnect bounds (`maxAttempts`, `delayMs`), shared semantics with `fetchServerSentEvents` — see [Advanced](./advanced). |\n| `WebSocketImpl` | Override the `WebSocket` implementation (tests, non-browser runtimes). |\n\nSee [Transports](../transports/overview) for where `webSocket()`\nfits among the other adapters.\n\n## Wire protocol\n\n| Direction | Frame | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Client → server | A `RunAgentInput`-shaped JSON object (same shape as the SSE/NDJSON POST body) | Start one `chat()` turn. |\n| Client → server | `{ \"type\": \"abort\", \"runId\": \"…\" }` | Abort one in-flight turn (see below). |\n| Server → client | `{ \"id\": \"…\", \"chunk\": <StreamChunk> }` | One chunk, tagged with a durability offset (only when `durability` is configured). |\n| Server → client | A bare `StreamChunk` | One chunk, untagged (no `durability` configured). |\n| Server → client | `{ \"type\": \"ping\" }` | Heartbeat. `webSocket()` drops these automatically; a hand-rolled client should ignore anything with `type: \"ping\"`. |\n\nThe two server→client shapes are unambiguous: the envelope never has a\ntop-level `type`, and every bare `StreamChunk` does.\n\n## Resume rides in the URL, not a header\n\nSSE and NDJSON resume with the `Last-Event-ID` header, because a `fetch`/XHR\nrequest can set arbitrary headers before it opens. A browser's `WebSocket`\nconstructor cannot set custom headers on the handshake, so the offset instead\nrides in the URL: `?runId=<id>&offset=<lastId>`.\n\n`webSocket()` handles this for you. If the socket drops before a run's\nterminal chunk (`RUN_FINISHED` / `RUN_ERROR`) and that run was durable\n(offset-tagged envelopes), it reopens at `?runId=&offset=` and de-dupes the\nreplayed boundary, the same reconnect guarantee `fetchServerSentEvents` gives\nyou, just carried differently on the wire. A run that never emitted an offset\n(no `durability` configured) has nothing to resume from: the drop surfaces as\na connection error instead of retrying forever.\n\nOn the server, a URL carrying `?offset=` is a resume, not a fresh turn. Route\nit to `resumeWebSocketStream`, a read-only replay of the durability log with\nno model call:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function handleResumeSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  resumeWebSocketStream(socket, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}\n```\n\n`resumeWebSocketStream` closes the socket with code `1008` if the request\ncarries no resume offset: there's nothing to replay.\n\n## Durability is per turn\n\nA conversation-scoped socket carries many turns, so its durability adapter\ncan't be built once for the whole connection. Each turn needs its own log,\nkeyed by that turn's `runId`. That's why `durability` in `toWebSocketStream`\nis a factory, not a value: it receives the per-turn `ctx` and reads\n`ctx.request`, whose URL already carries that turn's `?runId=`\n(`memoryStream`/`durableStream` key off it automatically).\n\n## Abort a turn\n\nAn `{ type: 'abort', runId }` frame aborts only that turn's `onRun` iteration\n(`ctx.signal` fires); the socket itself stays open for the next turn. The\nbuilt-in `webSocket()` client adapter sends this frame when the run's abort\nsignal fires (`stop()` in `useChat`), so the server actually stops generating\ninstead of running the model call to completion against a client that stopped\nlistening. A hand-rolled client should send the same frame to cancel a turn.\n\nClosing the whole socket aborts every turn still in flight on it.\n\n## Heartbeat and idle timeout\n\n`toWebSocketStream` sends a `{ type: 'ping' }` frame every `heartbeatMs`\n(default 30 seconds) to keep the connection alive through proxies that drop\nidle sockets. It closes the socket if no inbound frame arrives for\n`idleTimeoutMs` (default 5 minutes); a heartbeat itself doesn't count as\nactivity, only a client-sent frame does. The idle timeout never fires while a\nturn is still streaming, so a long single generation (an agentic loop, a\nturn longer than the timeout) is never cut off:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport type { WebSocketLike } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nfunction handleChatSocket(socket: WebSocketLike, request: Request) {\n  toWebSocketStream(socket, request, {\n    onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    heartbeatMs: 15_000,\n    idleTimeoutMs: 60_000,\n  })\n}\n```\n\n## Hosting on Node\n\nPlain Node (and anywhere else without a global `WebSocketPair`) has no\nbuilt-in way to accept a WebSocket upgrade, so you do it yourself and hand the\nresult to `toWebSocketStream`. The pattern: hook the HTTP server's `upgrade`\nevent, accept the socket with [`ws`](https://github.com/websockets/ws)'s\n`WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })`, and pass the resulting socket straight\nthrough. `ws`'s socket already implements the `send`/`close`/\n`addEventListener` surface `WebSocketLike` needs:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { WebSocketServer } from 'ws'\nimport { memoryStream, resumeWebSocketStream } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { Plugin } from 'vite'\nimport { handleChatSocket } from './handle-chat-socket'\n\nconst WS_PATH = '/api/chat-ws'\n\nexport function webSocketChatPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'websocket-chat-plugin',\n    configureServer(server) {\n      if (!server.httpServer) return\n      const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true })\n\n      server.httpServer.on('upgrade', (req, socket, head) => {\n        const url = new URL(req.url ?? '/', `http://${req.headers.host}`)\n        if (url.pathname !== WS_PATH) return\n\n        wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {\n          // `ws`'s socket satisfies WebSocketLike structurally — pass it\n          // straight through, no adapter needed.\n          const request = new Request(url)\n          if (url.searchParams.get('offset') !== null) {\n            resumeWebSocketStream(ws, { adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n          } else {\n            handleChatSocket(ws, request)\n          }\n        })\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is the same pattern used by the working\n[`examples/ts-react-chat`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/examples/ts-react-chat/src/lib/websocket-chat-plugin.ts)\nWebSocket example and by the e2e suite's\n[`durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts`](https://github.com/TanStack/ai/blob/main/testing/e2e/src/lib/durable-delivery-ws-plugin.ts).\nThe same wiring pattern applies elsewhere: accept the platform's socket, then\ncall `toWebSocketStream` / `resumeWebSocketStream` with it. A socket from\nDeno's `Deno.upgradeWebSocket` is WHATWG-shaped and satisfies `WebSocketLike`\ndirectly; Bun's `ServerWebSocket` (handler-object API) needs a small adapter\nfirst.\n\n## Hosting on Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare Workers (and Durable Objects) expose a global `WebSocketPair`, so\nyou don't upgrade anything by hand. `toWebSocketResponse` creates the pair,\naccepts the server half, wires it to `toWebSocketStream`, and returns the 101\nupgrade `Response` for you:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toWebSocketResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport default {\n  fetch(request: Request): Response {\n    return toWebSocketResponse(request, {\n      onRun: ({ messages, threadId, runId }) =>\n        chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId }),\n    })\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`resumeWebSocketResponse({ adapter })` is the matching read-only wrapper for a\n`?offset=` resume. Both throw with a message pointing at their `*Stream`\ncounterpart if called somewhere without `WebSocketPair` (Node, for instance),\nso there's no way to accidentally ship the Cloudflare wrapper to a runtime\nthat can't upgrade a socket itself.\n\n## Producer vs. socket lifecycle\n\nThe same caveat that applies to SSE and NDJSON applies here: with\n`memoryStream`, the run's producer and the delivery socket live in the same\nprocess. If that socket drops, the `chat()` call backing it aborts too, so\nreconnecting only replays what was already logged rather than resuming a run\nstill in progress. `durableStream` decouples the two (the producer runs\nagainst a backend, not the client's socket), so a drop there can reconnect to\na run that's still actively producing. See\n[memoryStream in production](./advanced#memorystream-in-production) for the\nfull explanation. It applies to WebSockets exactly as written there.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [Overview](./overview): the durability adapter contract this transport\n  reuses.\n- [Advanced](./advanced): reconnection bounding, offset ownership, and\n  Cloudflare Durable Streams deployment, shared across every transport.\n- [Transports](../transports/overview): where `webSocket()`\n  fits among the other client adapters.\n"}
